          "total_marks": 4,
          "stem_markdown": "The amount of time, in minutes, spent by a customer on one visit to a certain shop is modelled by the random variable \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{N}(10.5, 3.8^2)\\). In the past, the values of \\(\\mu\\) and \\(\\sigma\\) were 10.5 and 3.8 respectively. The shop has recently moved to a new location, and the manager hopes that the new value of \\(\\mu\\) will be greater than 10.5. He takes a random sample of 10 customers and notes the time they each spend in the shop. He then calculates the sample mean \\(\\overline{x}\\) for these 10 times.\n\nUsing a hypothesis test at the 5% significance level, the manager finds that there is sufficient evidence to conclude that the new value of \\(\\mu\\) is greater than 10.5.\n\nStating a necessary assumption, find the smallest possible value of \\(\\overline{x}\\).",
          "question_number": 5,
          "mark_scheme_items": [
            {
              "mark_code": "B1",
              "part_path": [],
              "is_final_answer": false,
              "sequence_number": 1,
